WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.110
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.030
Today, I’ve got some fluids for you!

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:12.090
Most hobby projects with fluid simulations
involve the simulation of a piece of sloshing

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.370
liquid in a virtual container.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:20.900
However, if you have a more elaborate project
at hand, the story is not so so simple anymore.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.880
This new paper elevates the quality and realism
of these simulations through using mixture

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.960
theory.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.289
Now, what is there to be mixed, you ask?

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:37.050
Well, what mixture theory does for us is that
it helps simulating how liquids interact with

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.600
fabrics, including splashing, wringing, and
more.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.330
These simulations have to take into account
that the fabrics may absorb some of the liquids

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:56.200
poured onto them and get saturated, how diffusion
transports this liquid to nearby yarn strands,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:05.189
or, what you see here is a simulation with
porous plastic, where water flows off of,

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:08.329
and also through this material as well.

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:13.159
Here you see how it can simulate honey dripping
down on a piece of cloth.

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:17.840
This is a real good one - if you are a parent
with small children, you probably have lots

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.979
of experience with this situation and can
assess the quality of this simulation really

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:23.979
well.

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:27.719
The visual fidelity of these simulations is
truly second to none.

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:29.189
I love it.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:34.829
Now, the question naturally arises - how do
we know if these simulations are close to

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:36.579
what would happen in reality?

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:42.259
We don’t just make a simulation and accept
it as true to life if it looks good, right?

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:47.929
Well, of course not, the paper also contains
comparisons against real world laboratory

00:01:47.929 --> 00:01:52.899
setups to ensure the validity of these results,
so make sure to have a look at it in the video

00:01:52.899 --> 00:01:54.259
description.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:58.459
And if you’ve been watching this series
for a while, you notice that I always recommend

00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:00.909
that you check out the papers yourself.

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:05.459
And even though it is true that these are
technical write-ups that are meant to communicate

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:10.970
results between experts, it is beneficial
for everyone to also read at least a small

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.170
part of it.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.060
If you do, you’ll not only see beautiful
craftsmanship, but you’ll also learn how

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:22.010
to make a statement and how to prove the validity
of this statement.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.010
This is a skill that is necessary to find
truth.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.629
So, please, read your papers.

00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:31.339
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

